course: dictionary
lesson: 13240
title: Meaning from the words around it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word «genuinely» tells you how something is done or felt. You can often work out what it means from the words around it. «I am genuinely sorry I broke your pencil.» The sorry sounds real, so genuinely must mean really and truly, not just saying it to be polite.
explain: Ask yourself: would the sentence still make sense without the word? «I am sorry I broke your pencil» still works, but it sounds less certain. Now swap in your meaning: «I am really and truly sorry» fits well, so you have found the right meaning.
explain: In stories, «genuinely» often shows a character's true feelings. «Mia was genuinely surprised when the box opened» tells you she was not pretending. The word helps you see what is inside the character, not just what she does.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Genuinely» means really and truly; honestly, not pretending.
fact: «Genuinely» can also mean in a way that is real and not fake, as in «a genuinely old coin».
fact: «Genuinely sorry», «genuinely surprised» and «genuinely interested» all show that the feeling is real.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «Sam was genuinely sorry for being late.» What does genuinely mean here? | Only a little bit | Loudly and quickly | *Really and truly, honestly | Pretending, just to be polite
exercise: «The shop sold a genuinely old coin.» What does genuinely suggest about the coin? | *Real and not fake | Very cheap | Lost and found | Shiny and new
exercise: Ella says, «I'm genuinely surprised!» What does she mean? | She is not surprised at all | She is very tired | She is pretending to be surprised to be polite | *She really is surprised, not pretending
test: «Dad was genuinely pleased with my drawing.» What does genuinely mean? | Quietly | *Really and truly | Once a week | Not at all
test: Pick the best meaning of genuinely in «She genuinely wants to help.» | Does not want to help | Wants to help, but only for a laugh | Helps very slowly | *Really and truly wants to help
exam: Jayden asks, «Does genuinely mean the same as really?» What is the best answer? | No, it means a colour | Yes, always, in every sentence | *Often yes: both can mean really and truly, honest and not pretending | No, it means something loud
